1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,906
Aarav pulls his charger out of the wall.

2
00:00:02,906 --> 00:00:06,279
But its little indicator light
keeps glowing for a moment.

3
00:00:06,629 --> 00:00:07,967
The power is gone.

4
00:00:07,967 --> 00:00:10,991
So where is that light
getting its energy from?

5
00:00:10,991 --> 00:00:11,925
A capacitor.

6
00:00:12,425 --> 00:00:17,097
A capacitor is two metal plates,
facing each other, with an insulator in

7
00:00:17,097 --> 00:00:17,997
between.

8
00:00:18,164 --> 00:00:20,647
The insulator is called the dielectric.

9
00:00:20,647 --> 00:00:23,984
It can be air, paper,
mica, plastic or ceramic.

10
00:00:24,334 --> 00:00:25,922
Its symbol is just that.

11
00:00:25,922 --> 00:00:28,999
Two short parallel lines,
with a gap between them.

12
00:00:29,649 --> 00:00:31,749
Let's charge one on the breadboard.

13
00:00:31,749 --> 00:00:34,831
A battery, a switch, a
resistor and a capacitor.

14
00:00:35,181 --> 00:00:36,363
Close the switch.

15
00:00:36,363 --> 00:00:39,585
Electrons rush from the
battery onto the bottom plate.

16
00:00:39,935 --> 00:00:44,294
At the same time, electrons leave the
top plate and flow to the battery.

17
00:00:44,294 --> 00:00:46,304
So the top plate turns positive.

18
00:00:46,654 --> 00:00:49,602
Notice that no electron
jumps across the gap.

19
00:00:49,602 --> 00:00:52,291
The dielectric does not let them through.

20
00:00:52,641 --> 00:00:53,908
Watch the scope.

21
00:00:53,908 --> 00:00:58,714
The capacitor voltage climbs fast,
then slower and slower, until it equals

22
00:00:58,714 --> 00:01:00,186
the battery voltage.

23
00:01:00,186 --> 00:01:01,874
Then the current stops.

24
00:01:02,524 --> 00:01:05,030
Now flip the switch over to a small lamp.

25
00:01:05,380 --> 00:01:08,401
The stored charge flows
back out through the lamp.

26
00:01:08,401 --> 00:01:11,899
The lamp glows, and the voltage
on the scope falls away.

27
00:01:11,899 --> 00:01:14,066
That is the charger's glowing light.

28
00:01:14,716 --> 00:01:17,356
So how much charge can a capacitor hold?

29
00:01:17,706 --> 00:01:20,835
Double the voltage, and
you double the charge.

30
00:01:20,835 --> 00:01:22,534
Q is proportional to V.

31
00:01:23,234 --> 00:01:25,185
So Q equals C times V.

32
00:01:25,185 --> 00:01:27,820
The constant C is the capacitance.

33
00:01:28,520 --> 00:01:30,121
C equals Q over V.

34
00:01:30,121 --> 00:01:33,577
Capacitance is the charge
stored for every volt.

35
00:01:34,277 --> 00:01:36,912
Charge in coulombs, voltage in volts.

36
00:01:36,912 --> 00:01:41,565
So capacitance is in coulombs per
volt, called the farad, after Michael

37
00:01:41,565 --> 00:01:42,465
Faraday.

38
00:01:42,945 --> 00:01:44,626
One farad is huge.

39
00:01:44,626 --> 00:01:49,996
Real capacitors are measured in
microfarads, nanofarads and picofarads.

40
00:01:50,646 --> 00:01:52,665
What decides the capacitance?

41
00:01:52,665 --> 00:01:55,121
Look at a parallel plate capacitor.

42
00:01:55,471 --> 00:01:58,924
Make the plates bigger, and
there is room for more charge.

43
00:01:58,924 --> 00:02:00,522
C grows with the area A.

44
00:02:00,872 --> 00:02:05,574
Bring the plates closer, and the opposite
charges pull harder on each other.

45
00:02:05,574 --> 00:02:07,596
C grows as the gap d shrinks.

46
00:02:07,946 --> 00:02:12,020
In air, C equals epsilon
nought times A, divided by d.

47
00:02:12,720 --> 00:02:17,231
Epsilon nought is the permittivity
of free space, eight point eight five

48
00:02:17,231 --> 00:02:20,401
times ten to the minus
twelve farads per metre.

49
00:02:21,101 --> 00:02:23,212
Now slide in a dielectric.

50
00:02:23,212 --> 00:02:27,652
It multiplies the capacitance
by K, the dielectric constant.

51
00:02:27,652 --> 00:02:30,722
So C equals K epsilon nought A over d.

52
00:02:31,372 --> 00:02:34,366
Bigger plates, closer
together, more capacitance.

53
00:02:35,166 --> 00:02:36,333
Worked example.

54
00:02:36,333 --> 00:02:41,033
Two plates, ten centimetres by ten
centimetres, are one millimetre apart

55
00:02:41,033 --> 00:02:41,933
in air.

56
00:02:42,134 --> 00:02:45,414
The area is zero point
zero one square metres.

57
00:02:45,414 --> 00:02:49,087
The gap is one times ten
to the minus three metres.

58
00:02:49,437 --> 00:02:54,285
C equals eight point eight five times
ten to the minus twelve, times zero

59
00:02:54,285 --> 00:02:57,514
point zero one, over
ten to the minus three.

60
00:02:58,214 --> 00:03:02,724
That is eight point eight five times
ten to the minus eleven farads.

61
00:03:02,724 --> 00:03:04,750
About eighty eight picofarads.

62
00:03:05,450 --> 00:03:10,518
Fill the gap with a dielectric of K
equals five, and it becomes about four

63
00:03:10,518 --> 00:03:12,589
hundred and forty picofarads.

64
00:03:13,239 --> 00:03:14,704
Now, the exam trap.

65
00:03:15,054 --> 00:03:19,213
Many students think a higher
voltage gives a bigger capacitance.

66
00:03:19,213 --> 00:03:20,271
It does not.

67
00:03:20,621 --> 00:03:23,704
Double the voltage,
and the charge doubles.

68
00:03:23,704 --> 00:03:25,615
Q over V stays the same.

69
00:03:25,615 --> 00:03:28,549
Capacitance depends only on A, d and K.

70
00:03:29,199 --> 00:03:30,762
Where is this asked?

71
00:03:30,762 --> 00:03:35,848
JEE Main and NEET ask for the new
capacitance when plates move or a dielectric

72
00:03:35,848 --> 00:03:36,748
goes in.

73
00:03:37,005 --> 00:03:42,408
PUC board papers ask you to define
capacitance and its unit, and to use

74
00:03:42,408 --> 00:03:45,360
C equals K epsilon nought A over d.

75
00:03:46,010 --> 00:03:46,911
Try this.

76
00:03:46,911 --> 00:03:51,781
A parallel plate capacitor has plates
of area zero point zero two square

77
00:03:51,781 --> 00:03:57,255
metres, half a millimetre apart, filled
with a dielectric of K equals four.

78
00:03:57,255 --> 00:03:59,519
It is charged to fifty volts.

79
00:03:59,519 --> 00:04:01,553
What charge does it store?

80
00:04:06,953 --> 00:04:07,853
B.

81
00:04:07,452 --> 00:04:09,939
About seventy one nanocoulombs.

82
00:04:10,289 --> 00:04:13,556
First, C equals K epsilon nought A over d.

83
00:04:13,556 --> 00:04:16,758
That is about one point
four two nanofarads.

84
00:04:17,108 --> 00:04:19,247
Then Q equals C times V.

85
00:04:19,597 --> 00:04:24,464
One point four two nanofarads times
fifty volts is about seventy point

86
00:04:24,464 --> 00:04:25,968
eight nanocoulombs.

87
00:04:26,618 --> 00:04:28,140
Screenshot this card.

88
00:04:31,340 --> 00:04:33,710
Next, capacitor types and ratings.

89
00:04:33,710 --> 00:04:37,088
Why does a capacitor carry
a voltage on its label?
